## STEP 2 (designed) -- enumerate the CLI surface, READ-ONLY, before sending any of it

Purpose: STANDING-ORDERS gate 10 -- check every command this case will send against the
platform before executing it, so no step fails on a parser rejection.  Done with
`qmark.py`, which types `PREFIX ?` with **no CR** and then clears the line with Ctrl-U; it
self-tests that nothing was executed first (`self-test OK: 'show clock ?' then Ctrl-U then CR
executed nothing`).  Never `<cmd> ?` through a CR driver, which would execute the command.

    tools/tb qmark tb277 cktac u2 115200 --mode 'configure terminal' -- '<each prefix>'

| prefix | completions offered |
| --- | --- |
| `tacacs-server ` | `host` Configure TACACS+ server / `key` Set shared key with TACACS+ servers / `timeout` Set time to wait for a server reply |
| `tacacs-server host ` | `A.B.C.D` Server IP address / `HOSTNAME` Server host name |
| `tacacs-server host 10.38.22.33 ` | `key` / `vrf` / `<cr>` |
| `tacacs-server host 10.38.22.33 key ` | `8` DES encrypted / `9` AES encrypted / `STRING` Key string |
| `tacacs-server timeout ` | `<1-1000>` Time in seconds (**default: 5**) |
| `aaa authentication login ` | `NAME` Named authentication method list / `default` Default authentication method list |
| `aaa authentication login default ` | `group` Use server group / `local` Use local username database |
| `aaa authentication login default group ` | `NAME` / `ldap` / `radius` / **`tacacs+`** Use all TACACS+ servers |
| `aaa authentication login default group tacacs+ ` | `group` / `local` / `<cr>` |
| `aaa authentication enable default ` | `group` / `local` |
| `aaa authentication enable default group ` | **`tacacs+` only** |
| `aaa authentication enable default group tacacs+ ` | `local` / **`none`** / `<cr>` |
| `aaa authentication enable default local ` | `<cr>` |
| `aaa accounting login default ` | `none` / `start-stop` / `stop-only` |
| `aaa accounting commands ` | `<1-15>` privilege level range |

Four surface facts worth keeping:

1. 🔴 **`tacacs-server host <ip>` takes NO port argument** -- `key | vrf | <cr>` only.  This
   is the fact that blocked the whole group until the Test Engineer started a server on the
   privileged port himself; it is re-confirmed here on the DUT rather than carried over from
   triage.  TACACS+ on this platform is locked to **TCP/49**.
2. **The shared key can be given as plain `STRING`, or pre-encrypted with `8` (DES) or `9`
   (AES).**  A plain-text key is stored back AES-encrypted (`key 9 ...`, STEP 5).
3. **`none` is offered for ENABLE authentication but never for LOGIN authentication.**
   `aaa authentication enable default group tacacs+ ?` offers `local | none | <cr>`, while
   `aaa authentication login default group tacacs+ ?` offers only `group | local | <cr>`.
   That asymmetry matches TFS S1599.1.071, which lists `tacacs+`, `local` and `none` for the
   enable method and no `none` for login.
4. **`none` is not reachable directly after `default`** -- it exists only as the fallback
   AFTER `group tacacs+`, so the TFS's "valid options will be tacacs+, local, and none" is
   realised as a method LIST, not as three peer keywords.

`tools/tb peek tb277 cktac u2 115200` afterwards: `/dev/u2 TAIL: 'awplus#'` -- the probe left
the console at a clean exec prompt, nothing executed, no configuration written.

**STEP 2 VERDICT: PASS.**  Every command this case goes on to send exists on this build.

## STEP 3 (designed) -- baseline OFF: logins happen and NOTHING reaches TCP/49

Purpose: `campaign-measurement-discipline` -- baseline the feature OFF first, so that any
packet seen later is attributable to the configuration and not to background chatter.

With **no `tacacs-server` line in the running-config** (`show tacacs+` before this step read
`No TACACS+ servers currently defined`), a 45 s capture was taken on the server's own NIC
while four real console logins were driven through it:

    tools/tb capture tb277 cktac eth3 45 tac-off.pcap tcp port 49      (in parallel with)
    tools/tb conlogin tb277 cktac u2 115200 --user manager  --password friend   --expect user
    tools/tb conlogin tb277 cktac u2 115200 --user ckabsent --password nosuchpw --expect fail

    LOGIN RESULT: USER  user=manager  seconds=0.4  prompt='awplus>'     -- MATCH
      conlogin: restore login as manager -> USER ('awplus>')
    LOGIN RESULT: FAIL  user=ckabsent  seconds=1.6  prompt='awplus login:'  -- MATCH
      conlogin: restore login as manager -> USER ('awplus>')

    tcpdump: listening on eth3 ...
    0 packets captured
    0 packets received by filter
    0 packets dropped by kernel

**STEP 3 VERDICT: PASS.**  Two tested logins plus two restore logins -- one of them a refusal
-- produced **zero packets on TCP/49** in 45 s.  The OFF baseline is silent, so every packet
counted from STEP 6 onward is caused by the configuration this case adds.

`ckabsent` was chosen for the refusal control in preference to a wrong password for a real
account: AW+ locks an account after 5 failed logins with a timer that restarts on each
attempt, and an unknown username touches no account's counter (`conlogin.py` limits, and the
dispatch's warning that group A locked swi_a's `manager`).

### Idle control, measured by accident and kept

A 40 s capture was taken **after** the TACACS+ server was configured (STEP 5) but with no
login driven through it, because of a shell-variable mistake in the tester's own command.
The result is worth keeping: `0 packets captured`.  **A configured-but-unused TACACS+ client
does not poll, keepalive or otherwise chatter to its server** -- the DUT speaks to TCP/49
only when an authentication is actually required.  Saved as `work/cap-idle.log`.

## STEP 4 (designed) -- build and PROVE the escape route, before the method list is touched

Purpose: `platforms/x960.md` §7E.1 says `local` at the end of a method list is a fallback for
an **unreachable** server and NOT for a Reject -- a server that answers Reject is honoured and
the local database is never consulted.  The TACACS+ server does not know `manager`, so the
instant the default login list points at TACACS+, **`manager` can no longer log in to this
DUT by any path**.  In group A the same trap cost a console login mid-run.  This case
therefore builds a proven way back BEFORE entering that state.

The escape route is a local `tacuser` whose LOCAL password differs from its TACACS+ password:

    awplus# configure terminal
    awplus(config)# username tacuser privilege 15 password localonly1
    awplus(config)# end
    awplus#

Proven immediately, while the method list is still `local`:

    tools/tb conlogin tb277 cktac u2 115200 --user tacuser --password localonly1
        --expect user --restore-user tacuser --restore-password localonly1

    LOGIN RESULT: USER  user=tacuser  seconds=0.4  prompt='awplus>'
    conlogin: expected USER, got USER -- MATCH
    conlogin: restore login as tacuser -> USER ('awplus>')

    tools/tb peek tb277 cktac u2 115200   ->   /dev/u2 TAIL: 'awplus#'

**STEP 4 VERDICT: PASS.**  `tacuser` logs in and `enable` raises it to `#`, because
`aaa authentication enable default local` reads the privilege 15 from the LOCAL database --
which this case never points at TACACS+ (see "NOT MEASURED").  That is the route back to
configuration mode in every state this case enters, and it is why the two passwords are
deliberately different: it makes STEP 7 a discriminator as well as a control.

