`00:50:03 UTC` + 13:00:15 = box **13:50:18 NZDT**, which is inside the capture window.

**Instrument 3 -- the wire.**  `tools/tb tool tb277 cktac tcpflow /tmp/cktac/tac-accept.pcap --port 49`:

    58 packet(s) total, 4 TCP flow(s) on port 49

    flow 10.38.22.33:49 <-> 10.38.22.34:42354  packets=17  duration=0.048s  => ESTABLISHED+DATA
       10.38.22.34:42354 -> 10.38.22.33:49  pkts=8  payload=66 B  flags={ACK, FIN.ACK, PSH.ACK, SYN}
       10.38.22.33:49 -> 10.38.22.34:42354  pkts=9  payload=46 B  flags={ACK, FIN.ACK, PSH.ACK, SYN.ACK}

    flow 10.38.22.33:49 <-> 10.38.22.34:42356  packets=12  duration=0.010s  => ESTABLISHED+DATA
       10.38.22.34:42356 -> 10.38.22.33:49  pkts=6  payload=71 B  flags={ACK, FIN.ACK, PSH.ACK, SYN}
       10.38.22.33:49 -> 10.38.22.34:42356  pkts=6  payload=30 B  flags={ACK, FIN.ACK, PSH.ACK, SYN.ACK}

    (and the identical pair again on ports 43604 / 43614 for the second login)

**Two logins produced exactly four TCP connections -- TWO PER LOGIN**, and the pair is
byte-for-byte reproducible across both logins:

| flow | out payload | in payload | reading |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| first, 17 pkts | 66 B | 46 B | the **authentication** exchange: an ASCII login is START -> REPLY(GETPASS) -> CONTINUE(password) -> REPLY(PASS), which is why the client sends more than it receives and why this flow lasts ~5x longer (0.048 s -- it waits for the driver to type the password) |
| second, 12 pkts | 71 B | 30 B | the **authorization** exchange, opened immediately after and lasting 0.010 s: a larger request (service/protocol AVPs) and a much smaller reply.  TACACS+ separates authentication from authorization -- the TACACS+ Feature Page says so explicitly -- and this is that separation visible on the wire.  It is what carries the server's `service = exec { priv-lvl = 15 }` back to the DUT |

Each connection is opened, used and closed (`SYN`, `SYN.ACK`, `PSH.ACK`, `FIN.ACK`), so the
client is not holding a single-connect session open.

**STEP 6 VERDICT: PASS.**  A user that has no usable local credential for the password offered
was admitted to the DUT, and all three instruments agree it was the TACACS+ server that
decided: the DUT logged `PAM-tacplus: User tacuser is authenticated`, `show tacacs+` moved
`Unknown` -> `Alive`, and the wire carried two complete TCP/49 conversations per login where
the OFF baseline carried none.  This satisfies TFS **S1599.1.010**.

### 🔥 A DUT-side discriminator for accept vs reject, available WITHOUT the key

The TACACS+ body is obfuscated with the shared key, so no instrument on the wire can read the
verdict.  It does not need to.  Compare STEP 6 with STEP 7 below:

| outcome | TCP/49 conversations per login |
| --- | --- |
| **accepted** | **TWO** -- the authentication exchange, then an authorization exchange immediately after |
| **rejected** | **ONE** -- the authentication exchange alone; no authorization follows |

The authentication flow itself is byte-for-byte the same shape in both cases.  **It is the
PRESENCE OR ABSENCE OF THE SECOND FLOW that reveals the verdict**, because the DUT only asks
"what is this user allowed to do?" once it has been told who they are.  That is a complete
accept/reject discrimination from a passive capture with no knowledge of the shared key, and
it is the instrument every control below is read on.

## STEP 7 (designed) -- discriminator: the SAME user's LOCAL password must be REJECTED

Purpose: close the loop on STEP 6.  `tacuser` exists in BOTH databases with DIFFERENT
passwords -- `testing123` on the server, `localonly1` locally.  If the local database were
being consulted, `localonly1` would work.

    tools/tb conlogin tb277 cktac u2 115200 --user tacuser --password localonly1
        --expect fail --restore-user tacuser --restore-password testing123

    LOGIN RESULT: FAIL  user=tacuser  seconds=1.4  prompt='Login incorrect'
    conlogin: expected FAIL, got FAIL -- MATCH

DUT log:

    2026 Oct 20 00:53:40 authpriv.err    awplus login: PAM-tacplus: auth failed: 2
    2026 Oct 20 00:53:41 authpriv.notice awplus login: FAILED LOGIN SESSION FROM ttyS0 FOR tacuser, Permission denied

Wire (`tac-reject.pcap`, 46 packets, 3 flows -- the refused login plus `conlogin`'s restore):

    flow ...:49 <-> ...:59448  packets=17  ESTABLISHED+DATA   out 66 B / in 46 B   <- the REFUSED login:
                                                                                      authentication ONLY
    flow ...:49 <-> ...:48444  packets=17  ESTABLISHED+DATA   out 66 B / in 46 B   <- the restore login:
    flow ...:49 <-> ...:48448  packets=12  ESTABLISHED+DATA   out 71 B / in 30 B      authentication + authorization

**STEP 7 VERDICT: PASS, and it re-confirms `platforms/x960.md` §7E.1 for TACACS+.**
The method list is `group tacacs+ local`, the local database holds a password that WOULD have
admitted this user, and it was never consulted: the server answered Reject and the DUT
honoured it.  `local` at the end of a method list is a fallback for an **unreachable** server,
not for a Reject -- measured here for TACACS+ for the first time, having previously only been
measured for RADIUS.  The DUT names the TACACS+ status code: `auth failed: 2`
(AUTHEN_STATUS_FAIL).

This is also the proof that STEP 6's login was decided by the server and not by anything
local, because both logins used the same account over the same path and differed only in
which database holds the password that was offered.

## STEP 8 (designed) -- a second authentication path, so the console is never the only way in

Purpose: STEPS 9 and 10 deliberately break authentication.  Breaking it from the console --
the only administrative path this bench has -- risks a state in which no credential works and
the only recovery is a reload, which this session forbids.  So a VTY path was added first and
every destructive leg was driven over it, with the console session held logged in at `#`
throughout (checked with `peek` before and after each one).

    awplus(config)# ssh server allow-users tacuser      (baseline was `ssh server allow-users manager`)

    tools/tb tool tb277 cktac netlogin --proto ssh --host 10.38.22.34 --user tacuser
        --password testing123 --login-timeout 40 --expect user --cmd 'show users'

    --- bytes after the password was sent (THE LOGIN BANNER) ---
    Last login: Tue Oct 20 00:53:48 UTC 2026 on ttyS0
    AlliedWare Plus (TM) 0.0.0 10/12/26 06:38:00
    awplus>
    LOGIN RESULT: USER  proto=ssh  user=tacuser  seconds=0.4

    === CMD: show users ===
    Line    User            Host(s)  Idle      Location        Priv Idletime Timeout
    con 0   tacuser         idle     00:00:07  ttyS0           15   10       N/A
    vty 0   tacuser         idle     00:00:00  10.38.22.33     1    10       N/A

Wire: 29 packets, the same **two** flows per login (17 pkts out 68 B / in 46 B, then 12 pkts
out 73 B / in 30 B).

**STEP 8 VERDICT: PASS.**  TACACS+ authentication serves the **VTY path as well as the
console**, with the identical two-flow signature.  Two incidental facts worth keeping:

* the `Priv` column of `show users` is the session's **CURRENT** privilege, not the user's
  maximum -- the console session reads 15 because it had been `enable`d, while this fresh VTY
  session reads **1** although the server returned `priv-lvl = 15`.  A TACACS+
  `priv-lvl = 15` does NOT auto-elevate the session; `enable` is still required
  (`platforms/x960.md` §7E.6 holds for TACACS+);
* the VTY login's request is 2 bytes larger than the console's for the identical credential
  (68 B vs 66 B) -- see the byte-length finding after STEP 13.

## STEP 9 (designed) -- control: a MISMATCHED shared key

Driven over SSH only; the console session stayed at `awplus#` the whole time
(`peek before: /dev/u2 TAIL: 'awplus#'`).

    awplus(config)# tacacs-server host 10.38.22.33 key wrongkey99

    netlogin --proto ssh --user tacuser --password testing123  -> LOGIN RESULT: FAIL     3.4 s
    netlogin --proto ssh --user manager --password friend      -> LOGIN RESULT: USER     0.3 s

DUT log:

    2026 Oct 20 00:56:44 auth.err awplus sshd-session[6911]: tac_authen_read_timeout: inconsistent reply body, incorrect key?
    2026 Oct 20 00:56:44 auth.err awplus sshd-session[6911]: PAM-tacplus: no more servers to connect
    2026 Oct 20 00:56:44 authpriv.notice awplus sshd-session[6911]: pam_unix(vty0:auth): authentication failure; ... user=tacuser
    2026 Oct 20 00:56:48 auth.err awplus sshd-session[6917]: PAM-tacplus: user not authenticated by TACACS+
    2026 Oct 20 00:56:48 auth.notice awplus sshd-session[6915]: Accepted keyboard-interactive/pam for manager from 10.38.22.33 port 55260 ssh2

Wire (`tac-wrongkey.pcap`, 24 packets, 2 flows -- one per attempt):

    flow ...:49 <-> ...:49706  packets=12  ESTABLISHED+DATA   out 41 B / in 72 B
    flow ...:49 <-> ...:51196  packets=12  ESTABLISHED+DATA   out 41 B / in 72 B

**STEP 9 VERDICT: PASS, with two findings.**

1. 🔥 **A wrong shared key has its own wire signature, and it is the INVERSE of a normal
   exchange.**  Normal: 17 packets, client sends more than it receives (66 B out / 46 B in),
   and a second authorization flow follows.  Wrong key: **12 packets, the server sends far
   more than it receives (41 B out / 72 B in), and nothing follows.**  The client gets one
   START away and never reaches the CONTINUE that would carry the password -- so **the
   password is never put on the wire at all** when the key is wrong.

2. 🔥 **A wrong shared key is treated as "server unavailable", and the `local` fallback DOES
   engage** -- `manager`/`friend` was admitted in 0.3 s.  Combined with STEP 7, this pins
   §7E.1 precisely for TACACS+: a *Reject* is honoured and local is skipped; a *protocol
   failure* is not a Reject, so local is used.

   🔥 **And unlike RADIUS, the DUT says WHY.**  `platforms/x960.md` §7E.4 records that for
   RADIUS a shared-key mismatch and a dead server are **indistinguishable** in the DUT's log
   -- both read `failed to respond`.  For TACACS+ the DUT prints
   **`tac_authen_read_timeout: inconsistent reply body, incorrect key?`**, naming the actual
   cause.  Compare STEP 11's genuinely unreachable server, which reads
   `tac_connect_single_vrf: connection failed ... Transport endpoint is not connected`.
   **The two failure modes ARE distinguishable on TACACS+ where they are not on RADIUS**, and
   that difference is a direct consequence of TACACS+ running over TCP: a connect failure and
   a decode failure happen at different layers.

   The fallback delay here was **0.3 s**, not the 25.5 s §7E.1 measured for an unreachable
   RADIUS server, because the TCP connection succeeded and the server answered at once.

The correct key was restored immediately afterwards and the escape route re-verified:
`netlogin --proto ssh --user tacuser --password testing123` -> `LOGIN RESULT: USER  0.4 s`,
console still `awplus#`.

### 🛠 Tool defect found and fixed in this step: `tools/netlogin.py`

The first run of the wrong-key leg returned `LOGIN RESULT: UNKNOWN`, not FAIL, although the
login really had failed.  Cause: a refused **ssh** login is re-prompted by the ssh client
(`tacuser@10.38.22.34's password:`), and `netlogin.py` only knew the **telnet** refusal shape
(a re-presented `<host> login:`, which group C had already fixed in T33378).  A control that
cannot report a failure is not a control, so the tool was fixed rather than worked around:
`post_pats` now maps a re-presented password prompt to `repassword` -> **FAIL**.

Regression-tested in STEP 10 below, which is a genuine refusal and now grades `FAIL` with
`--expect fail` matching, and against the three TACACS+-served ssh logins in STEPS 8, 11 and
13, which still grade `USER`.  `tools/README.md` updated in the same commit.

## STEP 10 (designed) -- control: a LOCAL privilege-15 account the server does not know

Purpose: the operational consequence of §7E.1, stated as a measurement rather than inherited
from group A -- and the regression test for the STEP 9 tool fix.

    netlogin --proto ssh --host 10.38.22.34 --user manager --password friend --expect fail

    manager@10.38.22.34's password:
    LOGIN RESULT: FAIL  proto=ssh  user=manager  seconds=2.0        (--expect fail: MATCH)

Wire (`tac-mgr-reject.pcap`): **17 packets, ONE flow**, out 64 B / in 46 B -- authentication
only, no authorization.  The reject signature of STEP 7, on the VTY path.

**STEP 10 VERDICT: PASS.**  `manager` -- a local account with `privilege 15`, offering its
correct local password, with `local` written at the end of the method list -- **is refused**
while TACACS+ authentication is active, because the server answers Reject for it.  This is
the single most operationally dangerous property of the feature and it is the reason this
case built an escape route in STEP 4 before touching the method list.

## STEP 11 (designed) -- multiple servers: sequential, in running-config order (TFS S1599.1.004)

`10.38.22.60` was chosen as the unreachable server after checking it is genuinely dead on the
same segment (`tools/tb ping tb277 eth3 10.38.22.60 2` -> 100% loss; `.40` and `.41` were
rejected because `.41` is somebody's live host).  It was configured FIRST:

    awplus(config)# no tacacs-server host 10.38.22.33
    awplus(config)# tacacs-server host 10.38.22.60 key testing123
    awplus(config)# tacacs-server host 10.38.22.33 key testing123

    awplus# show running-config | include tacacs
    tacacs-server host 10.38.22.60 key 9 kh3afeSqzrMfJtQogCRezF0CmP35QlDTDuTr0FNzz7I=
    tacacs-server host 10.38.22.33 key 9 kh3afeSqzrMfJtQogCRezF0CmP35QlDTDuTr0FNzz7I=

    awplus# show tacacs+
    10.38.22.60                                   Unknown
    10.38.22.33                                   Alive

    netlogin --proto ssh --user tacuser --password testing123 --expect user
    LOGIN RESULT: USER  proto=ssh  user=tacuser  seconds=3.6          (0.4 s with one server)

DUT log:

    2026 Oct 20 01:01:52 auth.err    sshd-session[7267]: tac_connect_single_vrf: connection failed with 10.38.22.60:49: Transport endpoint is not connected
    2026 Oct 20 01:01:52 auth.err    sshd-session[7267]: PAM-tacplus: connection failed srv 0: Transport endpoint is not connected
    2026 Oct 20 01:01:52 auth.notice sshd-session[7267]: PAM-tacplus: User tacuser is authenticated

Wire (`tac-two.pcap`, filter `tcp port 49 or (arp and host 10.38.22.60)`): **32 packets, of
which 29 are the two TCP/49 flows to 10.38.22.33** -- so **3 non-TCP packets, the DUT's
unanswered ARP requests for 10.38.22.60.  There is no TCP SYN to 10.38.22.60 at all**, because
ARP never resolved.

Afterwards: `show tacacs+` -> `10.38.22.60  Error` / `10.38.22.33  Alive`.

**STEP 11 VERDICT: PASS -- S1599.1.004 satisfied.**  Servers are tried **sequentially, in the
order they appear in the running-config** (the log even names the index, `srv 0`), the
unreachable one does not prevent the login, the second server serves it, and the failure to
reach the first is logged.  The cost is **3.2 s of added login latency** (3.6 s vs 0.4 s),
spent entirely in ARP resolution.  `show tacacs+` gains a third per-server state, **`Error`**,
alongside `Unknown` and `Alive`.

Two smaller facts: the two `key 9 ...` blobs are **byte-identical** for the same plaintext
key, so the stored form is deterministic and two servers sharing a key are visibly identical
in the running-config; and `10.38.22.33` read `Alive` again immediately after being removed
and re-added, so that state is not reset by reconfiguration.

## STEP 12 (designed) -- ALL servers unreachable: the local fallback

    awplus(config)# no tacacs-server host 10.38.22.33          (leaving only the dead 10.38.22.60)

    netlogin --proto ssh --user manager --password friend      -> LOGIN RESULT: USER  3.9 s
    netlogin --proto ssh --user tacuser --password localonly1  -> LOGIN RESULT: USER  3.6 s

DUT log:

    2026 Oct 20 01:03:44 auth.err    sshd-session[7450]: tac_connect_single_vrf: connection failed with 10.38.22.60:49: Transport endpoint is not connected
    2026 Oct 20 01:03:44 auth.err    sshd-session[7450]: PAM-tacplus: connection failed srv 0: Transport endpoint is not connected
    2026 Oct 20 01:03:44 auth.err    sshd-session[7450]: PAM-tacplus: no more servers to connect
    2026 Oct 20 01:03:44 auth.err    sshd-session[7450]: PAM-tacplus: user not authenticated by TACACS+
    2026 Oct 20 01:03:44 auth.notice sshd-session[7448]: Accepted keyboard-interactive/pam for tacuser from 10.38.22.33 port 55270 ssh2

**STEP 12 VERDICT: PASS.**  With every configured server unreachable the `local` fallback
engages and the DUT logs the exhaustion explicitly (`no more servers to connect`), which is
the logging half of S1599.1.004.

The second login is the cleanest possible proof that the fallback is genuinely LOCAL: the
account served was **`tacuser` with `localonly1`** -- the password that exists only in the
local database and that STEP 7 proved is ignored while the server is answering.  The same
credential that is refused with the server up is accepted with the server down.

**Measured fallback delay: 3.6 - 3.9 s**, against the **25.5 s** `platforms/x960.md` §7E.1
measured for an unreachable RADIUS server.  TACACS+ fails much faster because it fails at
ARP/TCP connect rather than waiting out a UDP retransmit schedule, so a console-login tool's
settle timeout does NOT need to be as generous for TACACS+ as for RADIUS.

## STEP 13 (designed) -- the decisive leg: a user with NO local account at all

Purpose: `tacuser` has a local account (the escape route), so a sceptic could argue the local
database contributed to STEPS 6 and 8.  `taclow` exists **only** on the TACACS+ server
(`priv-lvl = 1`) and has no local entry of any kind.

    awplus(config)# no tacacs-server host 10.38.22.60
    awplus(config)# tacacs-server host 10.38.22.33 key testing123
    awplus(config)# ssh server allow-users taclow

    netlogin --proto ssh --host 10.38.22.34 --user taclow --password testing123
        --expect user --cmd 'show users'

    --- bytes after the password was sent (THE LOGIN BANNER) ---

    AlliedWare Plus (TM) 0.0.0 10/12/26 06:38:00

    awplus>
    LOGIN RESULT: USER  proto=ssh  user=taclow  seconds=0.5

    === CMD: show users ===
    con 0   tacuser         idle     00:00:09  ttyS0           15   10       N/A
    vty 0   taclow          idle     00:00:00  10.38.22.33     1    10       N/A

Wire (`tac-taclow.pcap`): 29 packets, the two-flow accept signature
(17 pkts out 67 B / in 46 B, then 12 pkts out 72 B / in 30 B).

**STEP 13 VERDICT: PASS.  This is the case's decisive measurement.**  An account that does
not exist on the DUT in any form was admitted to its CLI, ran a command and was listed by the
DUT as a logged-in user.  Only the TACACS+ server can have authorised that.

### §7E.2's `Last login:` discriminator -- it DOES carry over to TACACS+, with a caveat

`platforms/x960.md` §7E.2 says a server-authenticated login prints **no** `Last login:` line,
because the line comes from the Linux lastlog of a *local* account.  Measured here for
TACACS+:

| login | local account? | `Last login:` in the banner? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `manager`, local (STEP 1) | yes | **yes** |
| `tacuser`, TACACS+ (STEPS 6, 8) | yes (the escape route) | **yes** |
| `taclow`, TACACS+ (STEP 13) | **no** | **no** |

So the rule is confirmed, and sharpened: **the discriminator is whether a LOCAL account
exists, not which protocol authenticated the login.**  `tacuser` was authenticated by TACACS+
and still printed the line.  Anyone using it to answer "who authenticated this?" must first
be sure the account has no local entry.

### 🔴 And a second caveat, measured by accident: `Last login:` can report a DELETED user's login

`tacuser`'s very first login (STEP 4, moments after `username tacuser` was created) printed:

    awplus login: tacuser
    Last login: Mon Oct 19 23:00:48 UTC 2026 from 10.38.22.33

`tacuser` had never logged in -- it had existed for seconds.  The record belongs to
**`ckalt`**, the temporary `privilege 15` account group C created and deleted earlier the same
day in T47199; `tb277/x960/mgmt-2026-10-20T0825/T47199/work/run1.log` records its telnet login
**from 10.38.22.33** and explicitly notes that `ckalt` showed *no* `Last login:` at the time
because it had never logged in before.  DUT `Oct 19 23:00:48 UTC` + 13:00:15 = box
**2026-10-20 12:01:03 NZDT**, inside group C's T47199 window.

⇒ **AW+ lastlog is keyed on the underlying UID, and a newly created username inherits a
deleted user's record when the UID is recycled.**  A freshly created account can therefore
report a last login it never made, from an access path it never used.  Reported to the
sentinel; it belongs in `platforms/x960.md` §7E.2, because it makes that discriminator unsafe
in exactly the situation campaigns create -- a session that adds and removes temporary
accounts.

### 🔥 The obfuscated body is NOT padded, so the packet length leaks both lengths

Five independent measurements of the client-to-server payload of the **authentication** flow
fit one model exactly, with no residual:

| step | path | user (len) | password (len) | measured out-payload | base + len(user) + len(pw) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 6 | console | tacuser (7) | testing123 (10) | **66 B** | 49 + 17 |
| 7 | console | tacuser (7) | localonly1 (10) | **66 B** | 49 + 17 |
| 8 | ssh | tacuser (7) | testing123 (10) | **68 B** | 51 + 17 |
| 10 | ssh | manager (7) | friend (6) | **64 B** | 51 + 13 |
| 13 | ssh | taclow (6) | testing123 (10) | **67 B** | 51 + 16 |

The console base is 49 B and the VTY base 51 B (the 2-byte difference is the `port` /
`rem_addr` attributes of the session).  Beyond that, **every byte of the username and of the
password is one byte on the wire.**  TACACS+ obfuscation is a keystream XOR and adds no
padding, so a passive observer who cannot read a single credential can still read **the exact
length of both the username and the password** of every login on the network.  This is a
property of the protocol rather than a defect in this product, but it is measured here on
this product and it is worth a line in the platform file: a capture of TACACS+ logins is not
as opaque as "the body is encrypted" suggests.

## STEP 14 (designed) -- removal

    awplus(config)# no tacacs-server host 10.38.22.33
    awplus(config)# aaa authentication login default local

    awplus# show tacacs+
    TACACS+ Global Configuration
      Source Interface    : not configured
      Timeout             : 5 sec

    No TACACS+ servers currently defined

Under a 45 s capture on TCP/49, with three real logins driven through it:

    netlogin --proto ssh --user taclow  --password testing123 --expect fail
        -> LOGIN RESULT: FAIL  proto=ssh  user=taclow  seconds=3.4
    conlogin --user manager --password friend --expect user
        -> LOGIN RESULT: USER  user=manager  seconds=0.4  prompt='awplus>'   (+ its restore login)

    0 packets captured
    0 packets received by filter

DUT log for the refused `taclow`:

    2026 Oct 20 01:05:41 authpriv.notice sshd-session[7709]: pam_unix(vty0:auth): check pass; user unknown
    2026 Oct 20 01:05:43 auth.err        sshd-session[7698]: error: PAM: User not known to the underlying authentication module for taclow from 10.38.22.33

**STEP 14 VERDICT: PASS.**  The feature removes completely: no server in `show tacacs+`, **zero
packets on TCP/49** under the same stimulus that produced 58, and the account that only the
server knew is now simply unknown (`pam_unix ... user unknown` -- note it is *local* PAM
answering now, with no TACACS+ line at all).  `manager` is admitted again at the local timing
of 0.4 s, so the §7E.1 lockout is lifted the moment the method list is restored.

